WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.725
Welcome to the Federal
Modernization Workbook.

2
00:00:02.725 --> 00:00:05.010
If you lead an agency's technology,

3
00:00:05.010 --> 00:00:08.275
security or workforce, this
is the place to start.

4
00:00:08.275 --> 00:00:12.944
In under ten minutes, I'll make one
case: that modernization should begin

5
00:00:12.944 --> 00:00:17.613
with identity, and that the first step
is small, bounded and can start now.

6
00:00:17.613 --> 00:00:22.613
There are four reasons, and then one specific
ask, with a finish line you can check.

7
00:00:22.613 --> 00:00:25.433
Everything I say is sourced
in the workbook's books,

8
00:00:25.433 --> 00:00:27.680
and I'll point you to them at the end.

9
00:00:28.430 --> 00:00:30.642
Here are the four reasons on one page.

10
00:00:30.642 --> 00:00:32.631
There are deadlines nobody can move.

11
00:00:32.631 --> 00:00:36.768
Every other decision depends on
identity, so it has to come first.

12
00:00:36.768 --> 00:00:39.892
The HR side of government
is being rebuilt anyway,

13
00:00:39.892 --> 00:00:43.855
which makes this the cheapest
moment to connect identity to it.

14
00:00:43.855 --> 00:00:46.793
And the access risk is
already on the books today.

15
00:00:46.793 --> 00:00:50.018
None of this requires a
large commitment to begin.

16
00:00:50.018 --> 00:00:53.500
Let's take them one at a time,
starting with the clocks.

17
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:57.837
Modernization is usually
treated as a choice of timing.

18
00:00:57.837 --> 00:01:01.126
It isn't, because the dates
are set outside the agency.

19
00:01:01.126 --> 00:01:06.550
Federal directives already require secure
cloud configurations and better logging.

20
00:01:06.550 --> 00:01:10.837
FedRAMP's new rules become
mandatory in January 2027.

21
00:01:10.837 --> 00:01:14.431
And the closest date is
Windows Server 2016,

22
00:01:14.431 --> 00:01:18.824
which reaches end of support
on January twelfth, 2027.

23
00:01:18.824 --> 00:01:23.538
In many estates that's where domain
controllers and certificate authorities run.

24
00:01:23.538 --> 00:01:26.963
Count your own 2016
servers before the meeting;

25
00:01:26.963 --> 00:01:29.774
that number will do more than any slide.

26
00:01:29.774 --> 00:01:32.560
So with time short, where do you begin?

27
00:01:33.310 --> 00:01:37.135
You begin with identity, because
everything else decides on it.

28
00:01:37.135 --> 00:01:40.455
Access policy, devices,
data, certificates:

29
00:01:40.455 --> 00:01:44.060
each one asks who or what
is making the request.

30
00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:46.789
If the answer to that
question isn't trustworthy,

31
00:01:46.789 --> 00:01:48.972
nothing built on top of it can be either.

32
00:01:48.972 --> 00:01:53.517
That's why the workbook numbers its
books in this order, with identity first

33
00:01:53.517 --> 00:01:58.135
and infrastructure next, then the cloud
boundary, assurance and operations.

34
00:01:58.135 --> 00:02:00.510
Skipping ahead only moves the problem.

35
00:02:00.510 --> 00:02:04.900
And right now there's an unusual
opportunity on the identity side.

36
00:02:05.650 --> 00:02:12.312
Federal HR 2.0 is moving agencies onto one
government-wide human resources platform.

37
00:02:12.312 --> 00:02:19.296
The OMB and OPM memo came in December
2025, Oracle won the contract in June 2026,

38
00:02:19.296 --> 00:02:23.551
and agencies move in
waves through fiscal 2027.

39
00:02:23.551 --> 00:02:28.013
Each agency's move includes new
connections, designed during onboarding.

40
00:02:28.013 --> 00:02:33.625
OPM rebuilds the HR side, but the
identity side is left to the agency.

41
00:02:33.625 --> 00:02:37.288
If identity rides along,
the agency connects once.

42
00:02:37.288 --> 00:02:39.438
If it waits, it builds twice.

43
00:02:39.438 --> 00:02:44.137
That's why the workbook calls HR
2.0 the opening, not the project.

44
00:02:44.137 --> 00:02:47.710
Which brings us to the risk
agencies already carry.

45
00:02:48.460 --> 00:02:51.736
Today, who gets an account,
and when access ends,

46
00:02:51.736 --> 00:02:55.098
often lives in tickets,
spreadsheets and email.

47
00:02:55.098 --> 00:02:58.648
A contractor roster is out of
date the moment it's saved.

48
00:02:58.648 --> 00:03:02.893
Quarterly access reviews can't see a
transition when it happens, so a review

49
00:03:02.893 --> 00:03:07.548
ninety days after someone leaves ends up
approving an account nobody should have.

50
00:03:07.548 --> 00:03:12.084
Assessors know this pattern well; it's
where account-management findings come from.

51
00:03:12.084 --> 00:03:14.561
HR-driven identity fixes the cause:

52
00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:19.598
the HR record becomes the source of
who works here, and access follows it.

53
00:03:19.598 --> 00:03:21.730
So what exactly is the ask?

54
00:03:22.480 --> 00:03:23.742
The ask is small.

55
00:03:23.742 --> 00:03:28.654
Phase zero is a four-week pre-flight,
and it doesn't require choosing a vendor.

56
00:03:28.654 --> 00:03:30.934
It needs three things: an inventory,

57
00:03:30.934 --> 00:03:34.705
from a read-only assessment of
the current estate; an owner,

58
00:03:34.705 --> 00:03:39.184
meaning a named executive sponsor
and a steward who runs the program;

59
00:03:39.184 --> 00:03:44.292
and a date, with the first gate on the
calendar next to those external deadlines.

60
00:03:44.292 --> 00:03:48.697
At the end of four weeks, leadership
sees the estate as it really is,

61
00:03:48.697 --> 00:03:51.143
and decides the next step from facts.

62
00:03:51.143 --> 00:03:53.860
Then comes the first real milestone.

63
00:03:54.610 --> 00:03:58.123
Phase one, the foundation,
runs about four more weeks

64
00:03:58.123 --> 00:04:01.635
and ends at a gate leadership
can check, not just sign.

65
00:04:01.635 --> 00:04:05.497
Groups and administrative units
are in place, with no drift.

66
00:04:05.497 --> 00:04:08.465
Everyone is under
multifactor authentication,

67
00:04:08.465 --> 00:04:12.173
except a few emergency accounts
that are vaulted and tested.

68
00:04:12.173 --> 00:04:15.248
The core infrastructure is
covered by address management.

69
00:04:15.248 --> 00:04:18.398
And the HR feed is live for
a first group of people.

70
00:04:18.398 --> 00:04:20.422
Each of those is measured from data.

71
00:04:20.422 --> 00:04:24.611
When the gate passes, everything else
has something solid to stand on.

72
00:04:24.611 --> 00:04:26.560
So who needs to be in the room?

73
00:04:27.310 --> 00:04:29.535
The decision belongs to a few people.

74
00:04:29.535 --> 00:04:35.160
The executive sponsor, with the CIO,
owns scope, funding and sequence.

75
00:04:35.160 --> 00:04:38.685
The steward owns the target
design and the early gates.

76
00:04:38.685 --> 00:04:41.848
And workstream leads say
when their area is ready.

77
00:04:41.848 --> 00:04:46.274
To make the conversation concrete,
bring four local numbers:

78
00:04:46.274 --> 00:04:51.852
how many 2016 servers you run, how many
contractors sit outside the HR system,

79
00:04:51.852 --> 00:04:56.898
how long it takes a departing person
to lose access, and your HR 2.0 wave.

80
00:04:56.898 --> 00:05:00.876
Then ask for one decision:
approve the four-week pre-flight,

81
00:05:00.876 --> 00:05:03.560
with a named owner and a start date.

82
00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:07.755
To go deeper, start with the
Book 00 executive briefing,

83
00:05:07.755 --> 00:05:11.047
which lays out the decisions
with owners and timing.

84
00:05:11.047 --> 00:05:16.097
Book 01, Chapter 12 is the roadmap,
with every phase and gate.

85
00:05:16.097 --> 00:05:19.415
Book 02 covers identity and the directory,

86
00:05:19.415 --> 00:05:23.585
and Book 03 makes the full
case for HR-driven identity.

87
00:05:23.585 --> 00:05:28.647
Every claim in this briefing is footnoted
to a primary source in those books.

88
00:05:28.647 --> 00:05:34.447
The workbook is independent analysis, not
an official publication of any agency.

89
00:05:34.447 --> 00:05:35.960
Thank you for starting here.
